(* EXPECT-ERROR: a string literal can be lent only to an RO parameter *)
(* This is a checked language that segfaults.  It was observed
   2026-10-01, in the released 0.13.0, while constant tables were being
   built.  A by-value STR parameter can be written through, a string
   literal is read-only data, and nothing connected the two.  This
   program passed the checker and died with SIGSEGV at the assignment,
   exit 139, no IndexError and no message.  The same held through an RO
   parameter lent onward to a writable one.  A count before the rule was
   chosen found three calls in four trees that handed read-only storage
   to a by-value slice, and none of the callees wrote.  So nothing that
   ran had met it; the 164 procedures that DO write through a by-value
   slice were all given real arrays.  The rule is at the call (par 2.4):
   read-only storage goes only to an RO parameter.  A STR has no
   subscript since it became the UTF-8 type (2026-10-31), so the
   writable slice here is a CHAR array, and the rule still holds. *)
MODULE m ;
PROCEDURE Up (s: SLICE OF CHAR) RAISES IndexError =
BEGIN
  s[0] := 'X'
END Up ;
PROCEDURE F () RAISES IndexError =
BEGIN
  Up ('abc')
END F ;
END m.
